{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.DerivationIterateLeibniz","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.DerivationIterateLeibniz","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Derivation.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.DerivationIterate.iterate_leibniz_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.DerivationIterate.iterate_leibniz_two","kind":"theorem","line":137,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/DerivationIterateLeibniz.lean#L137-L145","doc":"**The `n = 2` case, written out.**  `D²(fg) = D²f·g + 2·Df·Dg + f·D²g`.\nThe middle coefficient `2` is the first place the binomial content is\nvisible, so this is the smallest genuine test of `iterate_leibniz`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.DerivationIterate.iterate_leibniz_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.DerivationIterate.iterate_leibniz_one","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/DerivationIterateLeibniz.lean#L130-L135","doc":"**The `n = 1` case is Leibniz.**  Recorded as a consistency check that\n`iterate_leibniz` specialises correctly — a wrong binomial index would show\nup here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.DerivationIterate.exp_leibniz_iff_cross_vanishes","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.DerivationIterate.exp_leibniz_iff_cross_vanishes","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/DerivationIterateLeibniz.lean#L106-L128","doc":"**`exp(D)` is multiplicative exactly when the cross-term vanishes,\nat one-instanton order.**  For `D` with `D² = 0` the exponential truncates to\n`1 + D`, and\n\n`(1+D)(f·g) = (1+D)f · (1+D)g  ↔  Df · Dg = 0`.\n\nThis is the honest one-instanton statement behind \"the Stokes automorphism is\n`exp` of the alien derivative\": at first order the automorphism property is\n*equivalent* to the vanishing of the two-instanton cross-term, which is what\ntruncating at one instanton assumes.  The unconditional `exp(Δ)` automorphism\nneeds a completion or local nilpotency for the series to converge, and is not\nclaimed here.\n\nNote the `D² = 0` hypothesis is not needed for the equivalence itself —\n`1 + D` is the truncation whether or not `D` squares to zero — so it is not\ntaken as an argument.  It is recorded in the name because that is the setting\nin which `1 + D` *is* `exp D`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.DerivationIterate.iterate_leibniz","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.DerivationIterate.iterate_leibniz","kind":"theorem","line":48,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/DerivationIterateLeibniz.lean#L48-L104","doc":"**Higher Leibniz rule.**  Iterating a derivation `n` times across a product\ndistributes binomially:\n\n`Dⁿ(f·g) = Σ_{k=0}^{n} C(n,k) · (Dᵏ f) · (D^{n−k} g)`.\n\nThe `n = 1` case is `Derivation.leibniz`; the general case is the algebraic\nshadow of the analytic Leibniz formula, and it is what makes `exp(D)`\nmultiplicative.\n\nStated with `ℕ`-smul rather than a cast, so the inductive step needs only\nadditivity of `D` (`map_nsmul`) and never `Derivation.map_natCast`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}